427:
For purposes of this chapter, the following terms, phrases, words and their derivations shall have the meaning given below:
Motion picture, television and photographic production shall mean all activity attendant to staging or shooting (video-taping or filming) commercial motion pictures, television shows, programs or commercials, and to the taking of single or multiple photographs for sale or use for a commercial purpose where the photographer sets up stationary equipment on public or private property or the public right-of-way, in any one location for longer than five consecutive minutes.
Low impact film activities: In general, low impact film activities are defined as those which:
• Allow uninterrupted flow of pedestrian traffic;
• Hold vehicular traffic for no more than one minute intervals;
• Have minimal impact on normal business activities;
• Provide parking for cast, crew, extras and other nonessential vehicles in off-street lots, as well as an alternate parking for those displaced from these lots;
• Provide a private holding area for extras; and
• Conduct prep and wrap activities in accordance with the above.
High impact film activities: In general, high impact film activities are those that fall outside two or more of the criteria for a low impact activity. Additional criteria that classify a film activity as high impact include:
• High speed chases or crashes;
• Use of pyrotechnics or explosives; or
• Use of aircraft.
Garden City film coordinator shall mean the employee designated by the city council to work with and be the city's point of contact with media productions, and to assist in coordinating the permitting or authorization process for the city.
(Ord. No. 2016-6, § 1, 7-18-16)

481:
The notice to the owner shall meet the following requirements:
(1) The notice shall be in writing and mailed by certified mail to the owner's last known address or hand delivered;
(2) The notice shall include a summary of the animal control officer's findings that formed the basis for the dog's classification as a dangerous or potentially dangerous dog;
(3) The notice shall be dated and shall state that the owner, within 15 days after the date shown on the notice, has a right to request a hearing on the animal control officer's determination that the dog is a dangerous dog or potentially dangerous dog;
(4) The notice shall state that the hearing, if requested, shall be before the health officer;
(5) The notice shall state that if a hearing is not requested, the animal control officer's determination that the dog is a dangerous dog or a potentially dangerous dog shall become effective for all purposes under this article on a date specified in the notice, which shall be after the last day on which the owner has a right to request a hearing; and
(6) The notice shall include a form to request a hearing before the health officer and shall provide specific instructions on mailing or delivering such request to the agency.
(Ord. No. 2008-20, § 1, 9-15-08)

494:
(a) The City Manager or his designated representative shall enforce the following minimum standard codes enumerated in O.C.G.A. § 8-2-20(9)(B)(i)(I)-(VIII), to wit:
(1) International Building Code (ICC);
(2) National Electrical Code (NFPA);
(3) International Fuel Gas Code (ICC);
(4) International Mechanical Code (ICC);
(5) International Plumbing Code (ICC);
(6) International Fire Code (ICC);
(7) International Residential Code for One- and Two-Family Dwellings (ICC);
(8) International Energy Code Conservation Code (ICC); and,
(9) International Swimming Pool and Spa Code (ICC).
Said Codes, having mandatory statewide application and not requiring adoption by local ordinance, shall be enforced by the City Manager, his designated representative, or designee(s) thereof, in accordance with the procedures hereinafter set forth.
(b) The following optional state minimum standard codes enumerated in O.C.G.A. § 8-2-20(9)(B)(i)(IX - (XI), shall also be adopted and enforced by the City Manager or his designated representative or designee:
(1) International Property Maintenance Code (ICC);
(2) International Existing Building Code (ICC);
(3) National Green Building Standard (NGBS);
(4) International Building Code, Appendix N (ICC); and,
(5) International Residential Code, Appendix R (ICC).
(c) The Codes provided in subparts (a) and (b) of this Section shall mean the current edition of such Codes as approved by the State Board of Community Affairs, including any new edition of any such Code as provided in O.C.G.A. § 8-2-23(b); and provided, further, that any such Code may hereafter be amended or revised as provided in O.C.G.A. § 8-2-23(a). All references to "State Minimum Standard Codes" shall include the Georgia State amendments promulgated by the Board of Community Affairs and any local amendments approved by said State Board.
(d) Whenever the International Fire Code (ICC), as approved by the State Board of Community Affairs, is applicable, any State minimum standard code provision shall not be less restrictive than the Georgia State Fire Code, as adopted by the Georgia Safety Fire Commissioner pursuant to O.C.G.A. § 25-2-13.
(e) The City Manager or his designee shall make copies of relevant sections of the Codes enforced or adopted in this Section available to members of the general public, in printed or written format, at such price as deemed reasonable, to defray the costs of publication and handling. Due to copyright limitations, copying or duplication of some Codes may be restricted.
(f) In applying the aforesaid State minimum standard codes, the following standards may also be applicable.
(1) Part X of ANSI A17.1-1984, the America National Standard Practice for Inspection of Elevators, Escalators and Moving Walks, Inspector's Manual ANSI A17.2, the safety standards for manlifts ANSI A90.1a-1976, the safety standard for construction hoists ANSI A10.4-1981 and ANSI A10.5-1981, the safety standard for conveyors and related equipment ANSI B20.1-1984, or the latest revised rules and regulations adopted by the State Commissioner of Labor.
(2) Part 3, Fire Escapes, of O.C.G.A. Title 8, Chapter 2 (O.C.G.A. §§ 8-2-50—8-2-52) shall apply to all buildings within the City which are not used as private residences and which are three or more stories in height; and,
(3) Article 3 of O.C.G.A. Title 8, Chapter 2, "The Uniform Act for the Application of Building and Fire Related Codes to Existing Buildings" (O.C.G.A. §§ 8-2-200—8-2-222) is hereby incorporated by reference and shall be used by the City Manager or his designated representative whenever there exists the need to utilize compliance alternatives to any provisions of the rules, regulations, codes, or standards herein imposed or adopted.
(Ord. No. 2018-1, § 1, 3-5-18)
